  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nRest of today through Friday: Dry and breeze conditions will \ncontinue through this afternoon. Northwesterly winds will quickly \ndiminish this evening as the PBL decouples. With light winds tonight \npatchy areas of frost are possible across northern WI overnight with \nlow temperatures falling into the middle 30s. No Frost Advisory\nwill be issued for tonight as the growing season has ended across\nnorthern WI. Frost/Freeze headlines will resume for those areas \nafter spring green up.\n\nFriday will be another quiet weather day as high pressure continues \nto dominate the region. There is a low-end 10-15% chance that a \nstray shower or two could develop late Friday afternoon over east-\ncentral WI as isentropic ascent overspreads the region. Winds will \nalso veer around to the southeast Friday with peak gusts around 20-\n25mph during afternoon and evening \n  \n\nUnseasonable Weekend Heat And Strong Winds: \nAs surface high pressure shifts to the East Coast, an intense \nupper-level ridge will build across the western Great Lakes, \ntriggering robust southerly return flow. Daytime high temperatures \non Saturday and Sunday will climb into the upper 70s to middles 80s \nacross inland Wisconsin, approaching daily record high values at \nsome locations. Tightening regional pressure gradients will drive \ngusty southerly winds of 30 to 40 mph, with NBM probabilistic \nguidance indicating a 40 to 60 percent probability of gusts \nexceeding 35 mph on Saturday. \n\nEarly Week Cold Front And Cool Down: A cold front associated with a \nnorthern stream trough will traverse Wisconsin Monday. Rain chances \nremain on track to develop post frontal on Monday afternoon through \nMonday night. Mid-level frontogenesis will increase 24-hour \nprecipitation probabilities to 40 to 50 percent area-wide, though \ndynamic forcing remains progressive. NBM probabilistic guidance \nindicates a 20 to 35 percent (highest north of HWY 29) chance of 24-\nhour liquid accumulations exceeding 0.25 inches, with expected \nrainfall totals generally under a quarter inch. Post-frontal cold \nair advection will usher in a significantly cooler airmass for \nTuesday and Wednesday, dropping daytime high temperatures back into \nthe mid-50s to low 60s under partly sunny skies.",
